1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,883
How does a secant line become a tangent?

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:04,100
Let us zoom in, and watch the limit take

3
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:04,483
shape.

4
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:09,717
Picture a car moving down a road.

5
00:00:10,033 --> 00:00:13,433
Over a whole trip, average speed is straightforward: distance

6
00:00:13,450 --> 00:00:14,433
divided by time.

7
00:00:14,750 --> 00:00:16,967
But a speedometer answers a different question.

8
00:00:17,283 --> 00:00:19,233
How fast is the car moving right now, at

9
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:20,183
this exact instant?

10
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:22,650
To find out, zoom in on its position as

11
00:00:22,650 --> 00:00:23,467
time passes.

12
00:00:23,783 --> 00:00:26,800
A secant compares two nearby positions, and its slope

13
00:00:26,833 --> 00:00:28,383
gives the average rate of change.

14
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:31,533
Shrink the time interval toward zero, and that secant

15
00:00:31,550 --> 00:00:34,550
slope approaches the instantaneous rate, the derivative.

16
00:00:34,867 --> 00:00:36,617
Now let us see the same idea right on

17
00:00:36,633 --> 00:00:37,100
a curve.

18
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:42,883
Start with f of x equals x squared, a

19
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:44,000
familiar parabola.

20
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:45,733
Fix A at the point one, one.

21
00:00:46,067 --> 00:00:48,817
The first coordinate is x; the second is the

22
00:00:48,833 --> 00:00:49,717
function value.

23
00:00:50,033 --> 00:00:51,483
Now place B at three, nine.

24
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:53,150
Both points lie on the curve.

25
00:00:53,467 --> 00:00:55,383
A straight line through two points of a curve

26
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:56,083
is a secant.

27
00:00:56,417 --> 00:00:58,983
Its slope is rise over run: change in output

28
00:00:58,983 --> 00:01:00,333
divided by change in input.

29
00:01:00,650 --> 00:01:03,233
From A to B, the rise is nine minus

30
00:01:03,233 --> 00:01:04,150
one, or eight.

31
00:01:04,467 --> 00:01:06,450
The run is three minus one, or two.

32
00:01:06,767 --> 00:01:08,783
Eight divided by two gives slope four.

33
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:11,150
This is average change across the interval.

34
00:01:11,483 --> 00:01:13,200
Next, B will move closer to A.

35
00:01:20,100 --> 00:01:21,817
Name the horizontal gap h.

36
00:01:22,117 --> 00:01:24,700
Then B has x coordinate one plus h, and

37
00:01:24,717 --> 00:01:26,933
height the quantity one plus h, squared.

38
00:01:27,250 --> 00:01:29,867
The vertical rise is f of one plus h,

39
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:31,283
minus f of one.

40
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:34,650
At first, h equals two, so the slope is

41
00:01:34,650 --> 00:01:35,033
four.

42
00:01:35,350 --> 00:01:37,683
Now watch purple B slide along the blue curve

43
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:38,150
toward A.

44
00:01:38,467 --> 00:01:40,933
The pink secant pivots about yellow A, while the

45
00:01:40,933 --> 00:01:42,367
horizontal bracket shrinks.

46
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:45,300
At h equals one, the slope is three.

47
00:01:45,617 --> 00:01:47,767
At h equals one half, it is two point

48
00:01:47,783 --> 00:01:48,217
five.

49
00:01:48,533 --> 00:01:50,117
At one tenth, two point one.

50
00:01:50,433 --> 00:01:52,583
At one hundredth, two point zero one.

51
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:53,583
See the pattern?

52
00:01:53,917 --> 00:01:55,417
Every value approaches two.

53
00:01:55,733 --> 00:01:58,300
The vertical rise shrinks too, but the ratio of

54
00:01:58,300 --> 00:02:00,450
rise to run settles toward a steady value.

55
00:02:00,767 --> 00:02:03,467
For every nonzero h, A and B are still

56
00:02:03,483 --> 00:02:06,050
distinct, and the secant has an ordinary slope.

57
00:02:06,367 --> 00:02:08,417
We never set h to zero in that fraction.

58
00:02:08,733 --> 00:02:11,117
Instead, we ask what the slopes approach as B

59
00:02:11,117 --> 00:02:12,633
gets as close to A as we like.

60
00:02:12,950 --> 00:02:13,967
Their limit is two.

61
00:02:14,283 --> 00:02:16,033
The slopes do not need to equal two yet;

62
00:02:16,450 --> 00:02:18,167
only their limiting value is two.

63
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:32,350
Here is the algebra that explains the motion.

64
00:02:32,667 --> 00:02:34,817
The secant slope m is f of one plus

65
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:37,967
h, minus f of one, all divided by h.

66
00:02:38,267 --> 00:02:41,017
Substituting f of x equals x squared gives the

67
00:02:41,033 --> 00:02:45,017
quantity one plus h, squared, minus one, over h.

68
00:02:45,233 --> 00:02:48,100
Expand the square: one plus two h plus h

69
00:02:48,117 --> 00:02:50,350
squared, minus one, over h.

70
00:02:50,667 --> 00:02:52,767
The positive one and the negative one cancel.

71
00:02:53,083 --> 00:02:55,783
That leaves two h plus h squared, all over

72
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:56,117
h.

73
00:02:56,367 --> 00:02:58,717
Factor h from the numerator to get h times

74
00:02:58,733 --> 00:03:00,417
two plus h, over h.

75
00:03:00,733 --> 00:03:03,383
Since h is not zero, cancel the common h.

76
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:05,600
Now m equals two plus h.

77
00:03:05,917 --> 00:03:08,850
This cancellation is valid for nonzero h only.

78
00:03:09,167 --> 00:03:11,767
The original fraction at h equals zero would be

79
00:03:11,767 --> 00:03:13,117
zero divided by zero.

80
00:03:13,433 --> 00:03:15,650
Simplify first; then take the limit.

81
00:03:15,967 --> 00:03:18,967
As h approaches zero, two plus h approaches two.

82
00:03:19,283 --> 00:03:21,533
Therefore the derivative f prime of one is two.

83
00:03:21,867 --> 00:03:25,167
Geometrically, two is the secant slopes' limiting value.

84
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:31,450
Now watch B approach A.

85
00:03:31,767 --> 00:03:34,433
The pink secant pivots smoothly into a green tangent,

86
00:03:34,750 --> 00:03:36,483
the line with the curve's direction at A.

87
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:37,700
Its slope is two.

88
00:03:38,017 --> 00:03:40,350
On the slope triangle, one unit right is a

89
00:03:40,350 --> 00:03:42,633
run of one; two units up is a rise

90
00:03:42,633 --> 00:03:43,150
of two.

91
00:03:43,467 --> 00:03:46,483
Use point slope form: y minus one equals two

92
00:03:46,483 --> 00:03:47,850
times x minus one.

93
00:03:48,167 --> 00:03:51,083
Distribute, then add one, and we get y equals

94
00:03:51,083 --> 00:03:52,267
two x minus one.

95
00:03:52,583 --> 00:03:55,233
The secant connects two points and measures an average

96
00:03:55,233 --> 00:03:55,533
rate.

97
00:03:55,850 --> 00:03:58,067
The tangent is the limiting line at one point,

98
00:03:58,300 --> 00:04:00,967
and its slope is the instantaneous rate of change.

99
00:04:01,283 --> 00:04:03,800
That is the geometric meaning of the derivative: the

100
00:04:03,817 --> 00:04:05,550
exact rate of change at one point.

101
00:04:15,100 --> 00:04:17,550
Before we wrap up, let's quickly recap what we

102
00:04:17,567 --> 00:04:18,117
discovered.

103
00:04:18,433 --> 00:04:20,933
First, a secant line connects two points on a

104
00:04:20,950 --> 00:04:23,183
curve, giving the average rate of change.

105
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:26,733
Second, as the horizontal distance h shrinks toward zero,

106
00:04:27,117 --> 00:04:29,867
that secant line pivots into a single tangent line.

107
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:33,483
Finally, the limiting slope is the derivative—giving us f

108
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:36,183
prime of one equals two, and the tangent line

109
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:38,150
y equals two x minus one.

110
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:46,083
Thank you for watching.

